  "course": "advanced-econometrics",
  "title": "Advanced Econometrics",
  "description": "Sixty original constructed exercises for graduate econometric theory and selected field extensions. Prerequisites: undergraduate econometrics, graduate probability and mathematical statistics including laws of large numbers and central limit theorems, matrix algebra, multivariable calculus, constrained optimization and basic stochastic processes. Familiarity with conditional expectations, convergence in probability and distribution, and asymptotic order notation is required. The course examines theorem conditions, identification, estimator targets and nonstandard counterexamples rather than repeating introductory regression or causal-design recipes. Advanced extensions include dynamic-panel moments, support-boundary bootstrap failure, orthogonal nuisance rates and regularized inference. Numerical settings are hypothetical; official university materials guide coverage and are not copied question sources.",
